The menu bar must not flip a tall menu above itself, because there is nothing above it.

Driving iA Writer at a 1256x500 output, a path no gate had taken, put the whole View menu over the
title bar and the menu bar, covering the View title that opened it.

-[NSMainMenuView positionBranchForSelectedItem:screen:] hangs a branch below the bar item and, when
it would not fit, flips it to hang ABOVE instead. That is right for a pop up button in the middle of
the screen and never right for the main menu bar, which is at the top: there is no room above it by
construction, so the flip puts the menu off the screen.

MEASURED, the same View menu at three output heights, as the positioner receives it:

    684   asked=271,182  size=196x454  parent-top=684  local=271,48
    600   asked=271,98   size=196x454  parent-top=600  local=271,48
    500   asked=271,478  size=196x454  parent-top=500  local=271,-432

454 of menu plus 48 of chrome is 502, so at 500 it misses by two and the flip fires: the item top at
screen 476 plus the 454 height is 930, which is 430 above a 500 high screen. The compositor slid it
back on screen, which is why this read as a menu drawn in the wrong place rather than as a menu that
vanished.

Now the flip happens only when the flipped position is actually on the screen. After, with the other
two heights unchanged, which is the control:

    500   asked=271,-2   size=196x454  parent-top=500  local=271,48

so the menu hangs under its own title and runs two points off the bottom. macOS would scroll it
instead, which needs the arrows NSMenuView does not have here; below and clipped beats above and
gone.
--- a/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMainMenuView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMainMenuView.m
@@ -478,12 +478,17 @@
     topLeft = [[self window] convertBaseToScreen: topLeft];
 
     if (topLeft.y - branchFrame.size.height < NSMinY(screenVisible)) {
-        topLeft = NSMakePoint(itemRect.origin.x, NSMinY(itemRect));
+        NSPoint above = NSMakePoint(itemRect.origin.x, NSMinY(itemRect));
 
-        topLeft = [self convertPoint: topLeft toView: nil];
-        topLeft = [[self window] convertBaseToScreen: topLeft];
+        above = [self convertPoint: above toView: nil];
+        above = [[self window] convertBaseToScreen: above];
 
-        topLeft.y += branchFrame.size.height;
+        above.y += branchFrame.size.height;
+        /* ONLY IF HANGING IT ABOVE PUTS IT ON THE SCREEN. The menu bar is at the top, so for it
+         * the flip never can, and flipping anyway put the iA Writer View menu 432 points above the
+         * screen on a 500 high output. Below and clipped beats above and gone. */
+        if (NSMaxY(screenVisible) >= above.y)
+            topLeft = above;
     }
 
     if (topLeft.x + branchFrame.size.width > NSMaxX(screenVisible))
